+++
title: Baalbek
description: City in Baalbek-Hermel, Lebanon
hook: "Three limestone blocks in the western retaining wall of Baalbek's
  temple platform weigh approximately 800 tonnes (880 tons) each, and each is
  over 19 m (62 ft) long. Two still larger ones lie in a quarry 800 m
  (2,600 ft) from the town: the Stone of the Pregnant Woman, left unused, its
  weight estimated at 1,000 tonnes (1,100 tons), and a fifth stone of
  approximately 1,200 tonnes (1,300 tons)."
standfirst: Baalbek stands east of the Litani River in Lebanon's Beqaa Valley,
  about 67 km (42 mi) northeast of Beirut, and its history dates back at least
  11,000 years. Alexander the Great conquered the city in 334 BCE and renamed
  it Heliopolis, 'Sun City'. It flourished under Roman rule.
key-facts:
  - name: Where it is
    value: East of the Litani River in Lebanon's Beqaa Valley, about 67 km (42 mi)
      northeast of Beirut
    from: >-
      is a city located east of the

      [[ Litani River ]] in [[ Lebanon ]]'s [[ Beqaa Valley ]], about 67 km
      (42 mi)

      northeast of [[ Beirut ]].
  - name: What it is the capital of
    value: Baalbek-Hermel Governorate
    from: |-
      It is the capital of
      [[ Baalbek-Hermel Governorate ]].[[ ^6 ]]
  - name: How many people lived there
    value: 82,608, in 1998
    from: |-
      In 1998, the city had a population
      of 82,608.[[ ^7 ]]
  - name: How high it sits
    value: 1,170 m (3,840 ft) above sea level
    from: "elevation: 1,170 m (3,840 ft)"
  - name: Who is mayor
    value: Bachir Khodr
    from: "mayor: Bachir Khodr"
  - name: Why UNESCO lists it
    value: A World Heritage Site since 1984, under cultural criteria i and iv
    from: |-
      criteria: "Cultural: i, iv"
          reference: "294"
          inscription: 1984 (8th Session)
pull-quotes:
  - text: Baalbek, with its colossal structures, is one of the finest examples of
      Imperial Roman architecture at its apogee
    attribution: UNESCO, on making Baalbek a World Heritage Site in 1984
    section: excavations
  - text: No ruins of antiquity have attracted more attention than those of
      Heliopolis, or been more frequently or accurately measured and
      described.
    attribution: Donne, hyperbolising
    section: early-modernity
  - text: gates of palaces sculptured in marble and lofty columns also of marble
    attribution: said of Baalbek in the mid-10th century
    section: middle-ages-and-early-modernity
timeline:
  - when: 334 BCE
    what: Alexander the Great conquered the city and renamed it Heliopolis, 'Sun
      City'.
    from: |-
      [[ Alexander the Great ]] conquered the city in 334 BCE, he renamed it
      !!Heliopolis!! (Ancient Greek: Ἡλιούπολις,
      [[ romanized | romanization-of-ancient-greek ]]: //Hēlioúpolis//,
      [[ lit. | literal-translation ]] 'Sun City'
  - when: AD 193
    what: Septimius Severus granted the city ius Italicum rights.
    from: |-
      In AD 193,
      [[ Septimius Severus ]] granted the city //[[ ius Italicum ]]//
      rights.[[ ^80 ]][[ ^f ]]
  - when: AD 297
    what: The actor Gelasinus converted in the middle of a scene mocking baptism;
      the audience dragged him from the theater and stoned him to death.
    from: >-
      In AD 297, the

      actor [[ Gelasinus ]] converted in the middle of a scene mocking

      [[ baptism ]]; his public profession of faith provoked the audience to
      drag

      him from the theater and [[ stone him to death | stoning ]].[[ ^77 ]][[
      ^3 ]]
  - when: "379"
    what: The Temple of Jupiter, already greatly damaged by earthquakes, was
      demolished under Theodosius and replaced by another basilica, now lost,
      built from stones scavenged from the pagan complex.
    from: >-
      The Temple of Jupiter, already greatly damaged by

      earthquakes,[[ ^84 ]] was demolished under [[ Theodosius | theodosius-i
      ]] in

      379 and replaced by another basilica (now lost), using stones scavenged
      from

      the pagan complex.[[ ^85 ]]
  - when: sometime between 532 and 537
    what: Eight of the complex's Corinthian columns were disassembled and shipped
      to Constantinople for the rebuilt Hagia Sophia.
    from: >-
      Under the reign of [[ Justinian | justinian-i ]], eight of the complex's

      [[ Corinthian columns | corinthian-order ]] were disassembled and
      shipped to

      [[ Constantinople ]] for incorporation in the rebuilt [[ Hagia Sophia ]]

      sometime between 532 and 537.
  - when: AD 634, 636 or 637
    what: Baalbek was occupied by the Muslim army — the date is given as AD 634
      (ah 13), as 636, or as under Abu ʿUbaidah following the Byzantine defeat
      at Yarmouk in 637 (ah 16).
    from: >-
      Baalbek was occupied by the [[ Muslim army | rashidun-army ]] in AD 634

      ([[ ah | ah-era ]] 13),[[ ^85 ]] in 636,[[ ^35 ]] or under [[ Abu
      ʿUbaidah ]]

      following the [[ Byzantine | byzantine-empire ]]

      [[ defeat at Yarmouk | battle-of-yarmouk ]] in 637 ([[ ah | ah-era
      ]] 16),
  - when: "748"
    what: The Damascene caliph Marwan II sacked the fortified complex with great
      violence; it was dismantled and largely depopulated.
    from: >-
      but was sacked with great violence by the Damascene caliph [[ Marwan II
      ]] in

      748, at which time it was dismantled and largely depopulated.[[ ^87 ]]
  - when: "1260"
    what: The Mongolian general Kitbuqa took Baalbek and dismantled its
      fortifications.
    from: >-
      The [[ Mongolian | mongolian-empire ]] general [[ Kitbuqa ]] took
      Baalbek in

      1260 and dismantled its fortifications.
  - when: 10 May 1318
    what: "A flood destroyed the revived settlement: 194 people were killed and
      1500 houses, 131 shops, 44 orchards, 17 ovens, 11 mills, and 4 aqueducts
      were ruined."
    from: >-
      The revived settlement was again destroyed by a flood on

      10 May 1318, when water from the east and northeast made holes 30 m
      (98 ft)

      wide in walls 4 m (13 ft) thick.[[ ^105 ]] 194 people were killed and
      1500

      houses, 131 shops, 44 orchards, 17 ovens, 11 mills, and 4 aqueducts were

      ruined, along with the town's mosque and 13 other religious and
      educational

      buildings.[[ ^105 ]]
  - when: "1516"
    what: The Ottoman sultan Selim the Grim conquered Baalbek along with the rest
      of Syria.
    from: >-
      In 1516, Baalbek was conquered with the [[ rest of Syria | ottoman-syria
      ]] by

      the [[ Ottoman | ottoman-empire ]] sultan [[ Selim the Grim ]].[[ ^107
      ]]
  - when: "1759"
    what: The town suffered badly in the earthquakes, after which it was held by
      the Metawali.
    from: >-
      but the town itself

      suffered badly during the

      [[ 1759 earthquakes | near-east-earthquakes-of-1759 ]], after which it
      was

      held by the [[ Metawali ]], who again feuded with other Lebanese tribes.
  - when: "1835"
    what: The town's population was barely 200 people.
    from: |-
      In 1835, the town's
      population was barely 200 people.[[ ^126 ]]
  - when: 1 November 1898
    what: Wilhelm II of Germany and his wife passed through Baalbek on their way
      to Jerusalem; the archaeological team he dispatched began work within a
      month.
    from: >-
      [[ Wilhelm II | william-ii-german-emperor ]] of [[ Germany |
      german-empire ]]

      and his wife passed through Baalbek on 1 November 1898,[[ ^84 ]] on
      their way

      to Jerusalem. He noted both the magnificence of the Roman remains and
      the drab

      condition of the modern settlement.[[ ^84 ]] It was expected at the time
      that

      natural disasters, winter frosts, and the raiding of building materials
      by the

      city's residents would shortly ruin the remaining ruins.[[ ^104 ]] The

      archaeological team he dispatched began work within a month.
  - when: 19 June 1902
    what: Baalbek was connected to the DHP, the French-owned railway concession in
      Ottoman Syria.
    from: |-
      Baalbek was connected to the [[ DHP | beirutdamascus-railway ]], the
      [[ French | third-french-republic ]]-owned railway concession in
      [[ Ottoman Syria ]], on 19 June 1902.[[ ^143 ]]
  - when: 1943-11-22
    what: Wikidata dates Baalbek's country, Lebanon, from this day.
    from: |-
      country:
            - value: Lebanon
              from: 1943-11-22
  - when: March 1974
    what: Musa al-Sadr announced the launching of the "Movement of the Deprived"
      at a rally in Baalbek attended by 75,000 men.
    from: >-
      In March 1974, [[ Musa al-Sadr ]] announced the launching of the

      "[[ Movement of the Deprived |
      amal-movement#harakat-al-mahrumin-/movement-of-the-deprived ]]"

      in front of a rally in Baalbek attended by 75,000 men.
  - when: "1984"
    what: UNESCO made Baalbek a World Heritage Site.
    from: |-
      [[ UNESCO ]] reported in making Baalbek a [[ World Heritage Site ]] in
      1984.[[ ^139 ]]
  - when: "1984"
    what: Israel launched raids on Baalbek, killing 100 Lebanese civilians and
      wounding hundreds inside the city.
    from: >-
      In 1984, Israel launched raids on Baalbek, killing 100 Lebanese
      civilians and

      wounding hundreds inside the city.[[ ^154 ]]
  - when: 1 August 2006
    what: Hundreds of Israel Defense Forces soldiers raided Baalbek and the Hikmeh
      Hospital in Jamaliyeh to its north, in "Operation Sharp and Smooth".
    from: >-
      On the evening of 1 August 2006,[[ ^159 ]] hundreds of

      [[ Israel Defense Forces (IDF) | israel-defense-forces ]] soldiers
      raided

      Baalbek and the Dar al-Hikma[[ ^160 ]] or Hikmeh Hospital in

      Jamaliyeh[[ ^159 ]] to its north ("[[ Operation Sharp and Smooth ]]").
  - when: summer of 2014
    what: A German Archaeological Institute team led by Jeanine Abdul Massih
      discovered a sixth, much larger stone, estimated to weigh 1,650 tonnes
      (1,820 tons).
    from: >-
      In the summer of 2014, a team from the

      [[ German Archaeological Institute ]] led by Jeanine Abdul Massih of the

      [[ Lebanese University ]] discovered a sixth, much larger stone
      suggested to

      be the

      [[ world's largest ancient block |
      worlds-largest-ancient-block#monoliths ]].

      The stone was found underneath and next to the Stone of the Pregnant
      Woman

      ("Hajjar al-Hibla") and measures around 19.6 m × 6 m × 5.5 m (64 ft
      × 20 ft

      × 18 ft). It is estimated to weigh [[ ^141 ]]1,650 tonnes (1,820

      tons).[[ ^142 ]]
  - when: "2024"
    what: During its invasion of Lebanon, Israel bombed a restaurant frequented by
      tourists and damaged the historic Hotel Palmyra.
    from: >-
      During [[ its 2024 invasion of Lebanon |
      2024-israeli-invasion-of-lebanon ]],

      Israel bombed a restaurant frequented by tourists[[ ^141 ]] and damaged
      the

      historic [[ Hotel Palmyra | hotel-palmyra-baalbek ]].[[ ^171 ]]
