glossary:
  - term: Heliopolis
    gloss: The Greek name for Baalbek, "Sun City", used from the Hellenistic
      period and latinised under the Roman Empire, in reference to the solar
      cult there.
    from: >-
      Baalbek was called //Heliopolis// during the [[ Roman Empire ]], a

      [[ latinisation | latinization-of-names ]] of the

      [[ Greek | ancient-greek-language ]] //Hēlioúpolis// (Ἡλιούπολις) used
      during

      the [[ Hellenistic period ]],[[ ^30 ]] meaning "Sun City"[[ ^31 ]] in

      reference to the [[ solar cult | sun-worship ]] there.
  - term: Heliopolitan Triad
    gloss: "The three gods the local temples were primarily devoted to in the
      Canaanite period: a male god (Baʿal), his consort (Astarte), and their
      son (Adon)."
    from: >-
      the [[ Canaanite | canaanite-religion ]] period, the local temples were

      primarily devoted to the [[ Heliopolitan Triad ]]: a male god ([[ Baʿal
      ]]),

      his consort ([[ Astarte ]]), and their son ([[ Adon | tammuz-god ]]).[[
      ^55 ]]
  - term: Trilithon
    gloss: "The \"Three Stones\": a row of three limestone blocks in the second
      course of the platform's western retaining wall, each over 19 m (62 ft)
      long and weighing approximately 800 tonnes (880 tons)."
    from: >-
      The tallest

      retaining wall, on the west, has a second course of monoliths containing
      the

      famous "[[ Three Stones | trilithon ]]" (Ancient Greek: Τρίλιθον,

      //[[ Trílithon | trilithon ]]//):[[ ^52 ]] a row of three stones, each
      over

      19 m (62 ft) long, 4.3 m (14 ft) high, and 3.6 m (12 ft) broad, cut from

      [[ limestone ]]. They weigh approximately 800 tonnes (880 tons)

      each.[[ ^176 ]]
  - term: Stone of the Pregnant Woman
    gloss: A fourth, still larger stone lying unused in a quarry 800 m (2,600 ft)
      from the town; its weight is estimated at 1,000 tonnes (1,100 tons).
    from: >-
      A fourth, still larger stone is called the

      [[ Stone of the Pregnant Woman ]]: it lies unused in a nearby quarry
      800 m

      (2,600 ft) from the town.[[ ^177 ]] Its weight is estimated at 1,000
      tonnes

      (1,100 tons).[[ ^178 ]]
  - term: Baetylia
    gloss: Black conical stones revered by local cults as sacred to Baʿal.
    from: >-
      Local cults also

      revered the [[ Baetylia | baetylus ]], black conical stones considered
      sacred

      to [[ Baʿal ]].[[ ^67 ]]
  - term: Propylaea
    gloss: "The eastern entrance to the temple complex: a broad staircase rising
      20 feet (6.1 m) to an arcade of 12 columns flanked by 2 towers."
    from: >-
      The temple complex was entered from the east through the [[ Propylaea ]]

      (προπύλαιον, //propýlaion//) or Portico,[[ ^67 ]] consisting of a broad

      staircase rising 20 feet (6.1 m)[[ ^184 ]] to an arcade of 12 columns
      flanked

      by 2 towers.[[ ^84 ]]
  - term: Adytum
    gloss: The sanctuary of the Temple of Bacchus, 36 ft (11 m) deep, on a
      platform raised 5 ft (2 m) above the nave and fronted by 13 steps.
    from: >-
      The interior

      of the temple is divided into a 98 ft (30 m) [[ nave ]] and a 36 ft
      (11 m)

      [[ adytum ]] or sanctuary[[ ^193 ]] on a platform raised 5 ft (2 m)
      above it

      and fronted by 13 steps.[[ ^174 ]]
  - term: al-Qala'
    gloss: The name, lit. "The Fortress", under which the ruined temple complex
      was fortified after the Muslim conquest.
    from: >-
      The ruined temple complex was fortified under

      the name !!al-Qala'!! (lit. "[[ The Fortress | alcalá-surname ]]")[[ ^85
      ]]
further:
  - slug: heliopolis-ancient-egypt
    why: Baalbek was sometimes called Heliopolis in Syria, or Coelesyria,
      precisely to distinguish it from its namesake in Egypt.
  - slug: palmyra
    why: Baalbek sat on the route from Tyre to Palmyra, the position that ought to
      have made it wealthy from an early age.
  - slug: alexander-the-great
    why: It was his conquest that gave the town the Greek name it kept for a
      thousand years.
anchors:
  hook: >-
    The complex is located on a raised plaza erected 5 m (16 ft) over an
    earlier

    T-shaped base consisting of a podium, staircase, and foundation walls.[[
    ^i ]]

    These walls were built from about 24 [[ monoliths | monolith ]], at their

    lowest level weighing approximately 300 tonnes (330 tons) each. The
    tallest

    retaining wall, on the west, has a second course of monoliths containing
    the

    famous "[[ Three Stones | trilithon ]]" (Ancient Greek: Τρίλιθον,

    //[[ Trílithon | trilithon ]]//):[[ ^52 ]] a row of three stones, each
    over

    19 m (62 ft) long, 4.3 m (14 ft) high, and 3.6 m (12 ft) broad, cut from

    [[ limestone ]]. They weigh approximately 800 tonnes (880 tons)

    each.[[ ^176 ]] A fourth, still larger stone is called the

    [[ Stone of the Pregnant Woman ]]: it lies unused in a nearby quarry 800 m

    (2,600 ft) from the town.[[ ^177 ]] Its weight is estimated at 1,000
    tonnes

    (1,100 tons).[[ ^178 ]] A fifth, still larger stone weighing approximately

    1,200 tonnes (1,300 tons)[[ ^179 ]] lies in the same quarry.
  standfirst: >-
    is a city located east of the

    [[ Litani River ]] in [[ Lebanon ]]'s [[ Beqaa Valley ]], about 67 km
    (42 mi)

    northeast of [[ Beirut ]]. It is the capital of

    [[ Baalbek-Hermel Governorate ]].[[ ^6 ]] In 1998, the city had a
    population

    of 82,608.[[ ^7 ]] Most of the population consists of

    [[ Shia Muslims | shia-islam-in-lebanon ]], followed by

    [[ Sunni Muslims | sunni-islam-in-lebanon ]] and

    [[ Christians | christianity-in-lebanon ]];[[ ^7 ]] in 2017, there was
    also a

    large presence of

    [[ Syrian refugees | refugees-of-the-syrian-civil-war ]].[[ ^8 ]]


    Baalbek's history dates back at least 11,000 years. After

    [[ Alexander the Great ]] conquered the city in 334 BCE, he renamed it

    !!Heliopolis!! (Ancient Greek: Ἡλιούπολις,

    [[ romanized | romanization-of-ancient-greek ]]: //Hēlioúpolis//,

    [[ lit. | literal-translation ]] 'Sun City'; Syriac: ܐܝܠܝܘܦܘܠܝܣ,

    romanized: //ʾĪliyōp̄ōlīs//). The city flourished under Roman rule.
